"An infinite family of counterexamples to the Polycirculant Conjecture (https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.23423)\nciteturn2academia12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3 months ago; Date: Sun Jul 26 02:44:39 2026\n\nWe disprove the Polycirculant Conjecture, which states that every transitive 2-closed permutation group is non-elusive, i.e. contains a derangement of prime order.\nTitle: An infinite family of counterexamples to the Polycirculant Conjecture\nAuthors: Saul D. Freedman, Melissa Lee\nDate: Sun Jul 26 02:44:39 2026\n\nWe disprove the Polycirculant Conjecture, which states that every transitive 2-closed permutation group is non-elusive, i.e. contains a derangement of prime order. In fact, we prove a stronger result, answering a long-standing question of Marušič and Jordan: there exists a vertex-transitive graph admitting no semiregular automorphism. To do so, we employ recently developed methods of Chen et al. for constructing elusive groups via non-split extensions, allowing us to construct an elusive group $7^6.\\mathrm{PSU}_3(3)$ of degree 16,464. We show that this group is the full automorphism group of seven of its orbital graphs and hence is 2-closed. Our example extends to infinitely many counterexamples of the Polycirculant Conjecture, and infinitely many vertex-transitive graphs admitting no semiregular automorphism.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nRUL - Policiklična domneva in izmuzljive permutacijske grupe : magistrsko delo (https://repozitorij.uni-lj.si/IzpisGradiva.php?id=161683&lang=eng&prip=rup%3A25380013%3Ad4)\nciteturn2search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.1 years ago; Crawled: 3 months ago; MD5: 27C9E9E6A7F6C668175A72FE18E8DFD7 ... Title:  | Polycirculant conjecture and elusive permutation groups ... We introduce the polycirculant conjecture and show that none of the described constructions of elusive groups leads to a counterexample for this conjecture.\n\nLanguage:  | Slovenian\nKeywords:  | permutacijske grupe, policiklična domneva, izmuzljive grupe, vozliščno tranzitiven graf, semiregularen avtomorfizem\nWork type:  | Master's thesis/paper\nTypology:  | 2.09 - Master's Thesis\nOrganization:  | FMF - Faculty of Mathematics and Physics\nYear:  | 2024\nPID:  | 20.500.12556/RUL-161683 Image: Link is opened in a new window\nUDC:  | 512.5\nCOBISS.SI-ID:  | 207219459 Image: Link is opened in a new window\nPublication date in RUL:  | 13.09.2024\n\n## Secondary language\n\nLanguage:  | English\nTitle:  | Polycirculant conjecture and elusive permutation groups\nAbstract:\n---\nWe consider elusive permutation groups. Many examples of such groups are introduced, including the Mathieu group $M_{11}$ as an important example. We describe the known constructions of elusive groups and discuss the possible degrees of elusive groups. We classify all primitive elusive groups. We introduce the polycirculant conjecture and show that none of the described constructions of elusive groups leads to a counterexample for this conjecture. We also show that no transitive group of automorphisms of a vertex transitive graph of valency $3$ or $4$ is elusive. We also present some results on the highest order semiregular automorphism of a cubic vertex transitive graph.\nKeywords:  | permutation groups, polycirculant conjecture, elusive groups, vertex transitive graph, semiregular automorphism\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOn graphs with equal and different Kromatic symmetric functions - ScienceDirect (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0166218X26001186)\nciteturn2search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3 months ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; Volume 387, 15 July 2026, Pages 137-159 ... The Kromatic symmetric function (KSF) {\\overline{X}}_{G}${\\overline{X}}_{G}$ of a graph G$G$ is a K$K$-analogue introduced by Crew, Pechenik, and Spirkl in Logan Crew et al. (2023) of Stanley’s chromatic symmetric function (CSF) {X}_{G}${X}_{G}$. ... We disprove that conjecture by finding four pairs of 8-vertex graphs with equal KSF, as well as giving several ways to use existing graph pairs with equal KSF to construct larger graph pairs that also have equal KSF.\n\n## Discrete Applied Mathematics\n\nVolume 387, 15 July 2026, Pages 137-159\n\n# On graphs with equal and different Kromatic symmetric functions\n\nhttps://doi.org/10.1016/j.dam.2026.02.039Get rights and content\n\n## Abstract\n\nThe Kromatic symmetric function (KSF) {\\overline{X}}_{G}${\\overline{X}}_{G}$ of a graph G$G$ is a K$K$-analogue introduced by Crew, Pechenik, and Spirkl in Logan Crew et al. (2023) of Stanley’s chromatic symmetric function (CSF) {X}_{G}${X}_{G}$. The KSF is known to distinguish some pairs of graphs with the same CSF. The first author showed in Laura Pierson (2024) and Laura Pierson (2025) that the number of copies in G$G$ of certain induced subgraphs can be determined given {\\overline{X}}_{G}${\\overline{X}}_{G}$, and conjectured that {\\overline{X}}_{G}${\\overline{X}}_{G}$ distinguishes all graphs. We disprove that conjecture by finding four pairs of 8-vertex graphs with equal KSF, as well as giving several ways to use existing graph pairs with equal KSF to construct larger graph pairs that also have equal KSF. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nProblem 14A-4 (https://www.ibs.re.kr/ecopro/wp-content/uploads/2025/12/Communications-in-Combinatorics-pamphlet-20251212-15.pdf)\nciteturn2search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 10 months ago; In 1972, Chartrand, Kaigars and Lick [1] proved that there is a redundant vertex in a k-connected graph with minimum degree at least ⌊3k/2⌋. ... Meanwhile, Mader conjectured that the result would hold even if the path is replaced by any tree with the same order. ... When G is a bipartite graph, Luo, Tian and Wu [3] showed that the minimum degree condition in the conjecture above can be relaxed to k + m and proposed the bipartite version conjecture. ... Wu, Connectivity keeping paths in k-connected bipartite graphs, Discrete Math. ... 378 (2026) 125–135.\nProblem 14A-4\n\nThe redundant subgraphs in the k-connected graphs\n\nProposed by Yingzhi Tian, Xinjiang University\n\nFor a k-connected G, a vertex subset or a subgraph R of G is said to be redundant if G − R is still k-connected. In 1972, Chartrand, Kaigars and Lick [1] proved that there is a redundant vertex in a k-connected graph with minimum degree at least ⌊3k/2⌋. In 2008, Fujita and Kawarabayashi [2] conjectured that every k-connected graph with δ(G) ≥ ⌊3k/2⌋ + f_k(m) − 1 contains a connected subgraph G′ of order m such that κ(G − V(G′)) ≥ k, where f_k(m) is nonnegative. Mader [4] confirmed this conjecture and proved that f_k(m) = m. In addition, the connected subgraph G′ can be chosen as a path. Meanwhile, Mader conjectured that the result would hold even if the path is replaced by any tree with the same order.\n\nConjecture 1. (Mader [4]) For any tree T with order m, every k-connected graph G with δ(G) ≥ ⌊3k/2⌋ + m − 1 contains a tree T′ ≅ T such that κ(G − V(T′)) ≥ k.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAn infinite family of counterexamples to the Polycirculant Conjecture | Cool Papers - Immersive Paper Discovery (https://papers.cool/arxiv/2607.23423)\nciteturn2search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3 months ago; Crawled: 5 days ago; We disprove the Polycirculant Conjecture, which states that every transitive 2-closed permutation group is non-elusive, i.e. contains a derangement of prime order. ... Publish: 2026-07-26 02:44:39 UTC\n# 2607.23423\n\n## #1 An infinite family of counterexamples to the Polycirculant Conjecture [PDF^{1}] [Copy] [Kimi^{}] [REL]\n\nAuthors: Saul D. Freedman, Melissa Lee\n\nWe disprove the Polycirculant Conjecture, which states that every transitive 2-closed permutation group is non-elusive, i.e. contains a derangement of prime order. In fact, we prove a stronger result, a