provide some necessary conditions for the system to admit solutions.\nWhile a complete if and only if characterization appears out of reach at present,\nwe are certain that several additional interesting necessary conditions remain to be\ndiscovered.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nElusive groups from non-split extensions (https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.12652)\nciteturn2academia15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.2 years ago; Date: Mon Aug 18 06:32:49 2025 ... These groups are closely related to a longstanding open problem in algebraic graph theory known as the Polycirculant Conjecture, which asserts that no elusive group is $2$-closed.\nTitle: Elusive groups from non-split extensions\nAuthors: Jiyong Chen, E. A. O'Brien, Binzhou Xia\nDate: Mon Aug 18 06:32:49 2025\n\nA finite transitive permutation group is elusive if it contains no derangements of prime order. These groups are closely related to a longstanding open problem in algebraic graph theory known as the Polycirculant Conjecture, which asserts that no elusive group is $2$-closed. Existing constructions of elusive groups mostly arise from split extensions. In this paper, we initiate the construction of elusive groups via non-split extensions. This approach offers a key advantage: it allows the construction of elusive groups even when the acting group is not elusive. As a demonstration, we construct elusive groups of new degrees, namely $p^{3k-4}(p+1)/2$ for each Mersenne prime $p\\geq7$ and integer $k\\geq2$, along with the first odd degree $225$ and the first twice odd degree $450$. We conclude by proposing further problems to advance this new direction of research.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nHow to see the forest despite the trees - Bérczi‐Kovács - 2026 - Journal of the London Mathematical Society - Wiley Online Library (https://londmathsoc.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1112/jlms.70668)\nciteturn2search6 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2 months ago; Crawled: 2 weeks ago; For the problem of finding disjoint spanning trees, however, just figuring out a promising conjecture is already a great challenge. ... ## 6 TREE-CONNECTIVITY IN RIGIDITY THEORY ... Their proof relies on the constructive characterization of highly -tree-connected graphs, that is, -partition-connected graphs (see Theorem 5.6 for the special case ). ... Kriesell conjectured that a graph with high node-connectivity can always be partitioned into a spanning tree and a -connected subgraph [46].\nA framework is called redundantly rigid if it is rigid and remains rigid after the removal of any edge. For a multigraph , a so-called body-bar graph can be associated with . (Its definition can be found, for example, in [7].)\n\nConnelly, Jordán, and Whiteley [7] showed that the following are equivalent: (a) is generically globally rigid in , (b) is generically redundantly rigid in , and (c) is highly -tree-connected. Their proof relies on the constructive characterization of highly -tree-connected graphs, that is, -partition-connected graphs (see Theorem 5.6 for the special case ). We note that generic globally rigid body-hinge frameworks can also be characterized by highly tree-connected graphs [31].\n\nWe saw above that connectivity and constructive characterizations are closely related, and the latter turned out to be a useful tool in rigidity. Interestingly, this connection is bidirectional: recent results in rigidity were the key to prove some long-standing conjectures about connectivity. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nRedundant Connection: Graph interview question (https://dsatracker.tech/problem/redundant-connection)\nciteturn2search7 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 4 days ago; # Redundant Connection ... Updated 2026-09-29\n\nA tree with n nodes starts with zero edges and gains connectivity one edge at a time without ever closing a loop.\n\n# Redundant Connection\n\nA medium Graph interview guide. Task statement, worked examples, intuition, and step-by-step solutions.\n\n## The problem\n\nGiven an undirected graph that started as a tree with n nodes but has one extra edge added, find and return the edge that creates a cycle. The edges are given as pairs [u, v].\n\nExample 1\n\nInput\n    edges = [[1,2],[2,3],[3,4],[4,5],[5,1]]\n\nOutput\n    [5,1]\n\nWhy\n    Edges form a cycle 1-2-3-4-5-1. The edge [5,1] closes the cycle and is the redundant one.\n\nExample 2\n\nInput\n    edges = [[1,2],[1,3],[1,4],[2,5],[3,5]]\n\nOutput\n    [3,5]\n\nWhy\n    Edges form a cycle 1-2-5-3-1. The edge [3,5] closes the cycle and is the redundant one.\n\n### Constraints\n\n  * ·1 <= edges.length <= 1000\n  * ·1 <= edges[i][0], edges[i][1] <= edges.length\n\n## How to think about it\n\nUpdated 2026-09-29\n\nA tree with n nodes starts with zero edges and gains connectivity one edge at a time without ever closing a loop. As you stream through edges from left to right, test if both endpoints already share the same component. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nRooted Weyl Alternants for the Graceful Tree Conjecture — Metriq PRISM Laboratory (https://prism.metriq.org/papers/math-2026-10/)\nciteturn2search8 [wordlim: 200] Published: last month; Crawled: today; # Rooted Weyl Alternants for the Graceful Tree Conjecture ... Coefficients can then act as exact witnesses: a nonzero coefficient signals surviving labelings, while leaf recurrences and symmetry reductions remove large blocks of redundant work. ... Metriq PRISM Laboratory, Rooted Weyl Alternants for the Graceful Tree Conjecture, Metriq PRISM Laboratory Mathematics Research Paper MF-PRISM-MATH-2026-10, Version 1.2, 2026.\n\n# Rooted Weyl Alternants for the Graceful Tree Conjecture\n\nGlobal alternants, extremal-edge flows, and exact pruning tests\n\nMethod & partial progress MF-PRISM-MATH-2026-10 / v1.2\n\nAn exact signed-enumeration framework for graceful tree labelings, with global alternants, directed edge classes, and two leaf recurrences. The reconstructed finite audit reproduces 7,812 rooted types and the pruning results through thirteen vertices.\n\nRead the paper Release & files\n\nImage: Cover of Rooted Weyl Alternants for the Graceful Tree Conjecture\n\nCurrent public edition v1.2 · 2026-09-12\n\nIdentifier\n    MF-PRISM-MATH-2026-10\n\nSeries\n    Mathematics\n\nEdition\n    Version 1.2\n\nLength\n    9 pages\n\nReserved DOI\n    10.5281/zenodo.22728328 (record reserved)\n\n## What this paper is really saying.\n\nThe paper turns graceful tree labeling into an exact signed-enumeration problem that can certify and prune finite cases, but it does not prove that every tree is graceful.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nElusive groups and the polycirculant conjecture Pe (https://cameroncounts.github.io/web/talks/pre/bco.pdf)\nciteturn2search16 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6 months ago; 14 | Characterisations Theorem 3 (Giudici) A primitive elusive permutation group is isomorphic to the wreath product M11 K, for ( some transitive permutation group K (and so has degree 12k). ... 15 Isbell’s conjecture This problem arises in game theory.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nEuropean Journal of Combinatorics 124 (2025) 104091 (https://www.boa.unimib.it/retrieve/53edfb91-39ff-4b78-8be7-9d35f60c0f45/Barbieri-2025-Eur%20J%20Combinatorics-VoR.pdf)\nciteturn2search17 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6 months ago; circulant Conjecture: nontrivial 2-closed transitive permutation groups contain nontrivial semiregu- ... Polycirculant Conjecture for such graphs. ... https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ejc.2024.104091\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOn certain edge-transitive bicirculants (https://emis.de/ft/31355)\nciteturn2search18 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7.6 years ago; of resear